A common real pattern: catch Ctrl+C, flip a flag, and let the main loop exit cleanly instead of dying mid-write.

```c
#include <signal.h>
#include <stdio.h>

volatile sig_atomic_t stop = 0;

void handle_sigint(int sig) {
    stop = 1;  // only this is guaranteed safe here
}

int main(void) {
    struct sigaction sa = {0};
    sa.sa_handler = handle_sigint;
    sigaction(SIGINT, &sa, NULL);

    while (!stop) {
        // do work
    }
    printf("flushing state, exiting cleanly\n");
    return 0;
}
```

The handler does the absolute minimum — set a `volatile sig_atomic_t` flag — and all real cleanup (closing files, flushing buffers) happens back in `main()`'s normal flow, outside the handler. That split is the pattern to memorize, not just this snippet.
